2. @smogon/calc (used as a dev-only correctness oracle, and as the source of the
   Champions game-data dump in poke_calc/poke_calc/data/dump/)
   Source: https://github.com/smogon/damage-calc
   License: MIT
   Pinned commit: 636e5b9bb42aa374e99323cdeec0317f7f8a16e0 (2026-08-21)

   The Champions mode ("generation 0") used by this project is not present in
   the published npm package (@smogon/calc@0.11.0) as of the pinned date; the
   oracle/ directory clones and builds the repository from the pinned commit
   directly. If refreshing this pin, re-verify the data dump and re-run the
   differential fuzz suite before trusting new results.

   MIT License text: see oracle/LICENSE (identical structure to poke-env's, with
   copyright held by the smogon/damage-calc contributors).

   Known data gap in the pinned build: @smogon/calc's own CHAMPIONS_PATCH merge
   is shallow (not deep) for exactly 12 move ids, so the patch object (which
   only carries the *changed* fields) replaces the base SV move entry instead
   of merging into it. This drops `type` (and, for Metal Claw, `basePower`)
   entirely. Confirmed upstream, not a dump artifact: calling calculate() with
   any of these moves throws in the pinned build. Affected ids: anchorshot,
   astralbarrage, bloodmoon, boltbeak, dragonhammer, fishiousrend, geargrind,
   hyperdrill, metalclaw, revelationdance, snipeshot, tripledive. Only the
   first four (plus metalclaw's slicing flag) are legal in Champions today;
   the rest are "Past"-flagged and dormant. poke_calc/poke_calc/data/loader.py patches
   all 12 with correct types (and Metal Claw's base power) from well-known,
   non-Champions-specific game data. Because calculate() itself throws on
   these upstream, they must be excluded from any oracle-diff fuzzing
   (Phase 3) and are instead covered by hand-verified unit tests
   (tests/test_loader.py::TestMoveDataGapPatch).

   Second known gap: the pinned oracle build throws `damage[damage.length -
   1] === 0` (an internal assertion, not a data problem) for ANY case whose
   correct answer is total type immunity - reproduced with both a
   Champions-exclusive ability (Eelevate) and a plain SV ability (Levitate)
   vs an Earthquake, so this is general, not ability-specific. Champions
   engine code (poke_calc/poke_calc/engine/damage.py) returns [0]*16 for these cases,
   which is correct per the type chart / immunity mechanics independent of
   the oracle; such cases are verified by direct unit test instead of
   oracle diffing (tests/test_differential.py's
   test_eelevate_grounds_ground_move_immunity) and should be excluded from
   Phase 3's fuzz corpus the same way (or the fuzzer should treat an oracle
   `ok: false` response as "skip, not a real mismatch" rather than a
   failure - see tests/test_differential.py::TestRandomizedSweep for the
   pattern this should generalize).

3. Pokekipe (poke_calc/poke_calc/data/dump/pokekipe_sets.json - the suggested nature +
   SP spread + held item shown by default for each species in the UI)
   Source: https://pokekipe.com (free public API, no account needed;
   OpenAPI spec at https://pokekipe.com/openapi.json)
   License/terms: none stated in the OpenAPI spec at the time of fetching;
   this project only reads the free public read endpoints and does not
   redistribute anything beyond a small per-species (nature, SP spread,
   item) summary derived from them.

   Snapshot taken 2026-08-31 via poke_calc/poke_calc/data/generate_pokekipe_sets.py,
   which fetches /api/v1/pokemon/{species-slug}/{format_id}?tolerate_missing=true
   for every dex species, from gen9championsvgc2026regmb (Champions VGC
   2026 Reg M-B, the biggest Doubles/VGC format). Doubles-only by request -
   the suggested set shown in the UI is always the Doubles ladder spread,
   regardless of the Singles/Doubles toggle elsewhere in the UI (an earlier
   version of this snapshot also fetched Champions OU for a Singles-specific
   suggestion; that was dropped, and the JSON shape simplified from
   {species: {singles, doubles}} to {species: spread|null} accordingly).
   Only the top-usage entry from each response's `spreads` list (and,
   since 2026-08-31, the top entry from its `items` list) is kept. As of
   this snapshot 304/324 species have data; the rest (mostly niche/newer
   Mega formes, plus a handful that hit the API's rate limit during the
   fetch itself - see generate_pokekipe_sets.py's _fetch_json docstring)
   fall back to poke_calc/poke_calc/engine/suggestions.py's base-stat heuristic for
   nature/SP (that heuristic has no item-suggestion equivalent, so those
   species just default to no item) - see that module's docstring for what
   the heuristic is and isn't. Re-run the generator periodically to pick
   up ladder shifts; it is NOT called live from the running UI (Streamlit
   reruns the whole script on every widget interaction, so a live
   per-species call there would mean hundreds of requests to Pokekipe's
   free service per session).

   Confirmed by direct API inspection, not assumed: species slugs on
   Pokekipe are the display name lowercased with hyphens kept as-is (e.g.
   "Tauros-Paldea-Aqua" -> "tauros-paldea-aqua") - a third, different
   convention from both this project's own to_id() and from Showdown's
   sprite filenames (see poke_calc/poke_calc/data/generate_sprite_ids.py). The `spread` string
   ("Nature:hp/atk/def/spa/spd/spe") is already in the 0-32 SP scale this
   project uses internally, not the mainline 0-252 EV scale - no
   conversion was needed. Unlike species slugs, `items` list entries'
   `name` fields ARE already in this project's own to_id() convention
   (e.g. "lifeorb") - confirmed by direct inspection, no remapping needed.
   The suggested item is the raw top-usage one from this Doubles ladder
   snapshot; it is not filtered for legality under Regulation M-A - the UI
   falls back to "(none)" itself if it isn't legal (see ui/app.py). For a
   Mega forme species this is simply that Mega's own Stone (that ladder
   entry's near-100%-usage item, since the forme requires it) - harmless,
   since the UI hardcodes the Mega Stone for Mega formes independently of
   this suggestion anyway.

4. Showdown's base data/pokedex.ts (poke_calc/poke_calc/data/dump/ability_slots.json -
   which abilities each species can actually have, used to restrict the
   UI's Ability dropdown)
   Source: https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown (unmodified base
   dex, NOT the champions mod - there is no champions/pokedex.ts override)
   License: MIT (smogon/pokemon-showdown)

   @smogon/calc's own species data (this project's main dex) records only
   ONE ability per species - not a Champions mechanic, just a consequence
   of @smogon/calc being a calculator rather than a team validator (its
   own UI lets you type any ability regardless of species). Champions
   itself DOES restrict ability choice to a species' normal Slot 0/1/
   Hidden pool (confirmed: its ruleset inherits "Obtainable Abilities" via
   data/mods/champions/rulesets.ts's `standardag` -> `Standard AG` chain),
   so poke_calc/poke_calc/data/generate_ability_slots.py fetches pokedex.ts directly
   for the real per-species slot data - present there even for Champions-
   exclusive Mega formes, which live in the unmodified base dex tagged
   `isNonstandard: "Future"` until a regulation enables them.

   Cross-checking the two sources surfaced a genuine discrepancy for 2 of
   324 species: @smogon/calc's species.json says Hawlucha-Mega's ability
   is "Limber" and Skarmory-Mega's is "Keen Eye"; pokedex.ts (and this
   project's own earlier, independently-sourced Mega research) says "No
   Guard" and "Stalwart". pokedex.ts is treated as authoritative - see
   generate_ability_slots.py's docstring and Dex.ability_slots()'s.

   One ability, "Battle Bond", appears in pokedex.ts but not in
   @smogon/calc's own 200-ability Champions table - excluded from
   ability_slots() results since this project has no mechanical data to
   simulate it with (same handling as "Pound" for learnsets - see #2
   above's move-data-gap entries and generate_learnsets.py).

5. Showdown's base data/text/abilities.ts (poke_calc/poke_calc/data/dump/
   ability_descriptions.json - the human-readable description shown for
   each ability in the UI)
   Source: https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown (unmodified base
   dex's text tree, NOT the champions mod - there is no
   champions/text/abilities.ts override)
   License: MIT (smogon/pokemon-showdown)

   @smogon/calc's own abilities.json dump (data/dump/abilities.json, this
   project's main ability table) carries only id/name - no prose, since a
   damage calculator has no mechanical need for it. Showdown's separate
   data/text/ tree exists purely to supply this: id -> {name, shortDesc,
   desc, ...}. poke_calc/poke_calc/data/generate_ability_descriptions.py fetches it
   directly and keeps shortDesc (falling back to the longer desc for the
   handful of abilities that only have that) for every id in this dex's
   200-ability Champions table - 200/200 resolved, including the 6
   Champions-exclusive abilities (Mega Sol, Fire Mane, Dragonize,
   Eelevate, Piercing Drill, Spicy Spray), which Showdown's text tree
   already carries text for despite living in the unmodified base file.
   Ability ids there already match this project's own to_id() convention
   directly, unlike species/sprite/Pokekipe slugs elsewhere in
   data/generate_*.py, so no fuzzy fallback matching was needed here.

6. Showdown's base data/text/moves.ts (poke_calc/poke_calc/data/dump/
   move_descriptions.json - the full human-readable description shown for
   each move in the UI)
   Source: https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown (unmodified base
   dex's text tree, NOT the champions mod - there is no
   champions/text/moves.ts override)
   License: MIT (smogon/pokemon-showdown)

   Same rationale as #5: @smogon/calc's own moves.json dump carries only
   the mechanical fields a damage calculator needs, no prose.
   poke_calc/poke_calc/data/generate_move_descriptions.py fetches Showdown's
   data/text/moves.ts and keeps the longer `desc` field (falling back to
   shortDesc only for moves that lack one) for every id in this dex's
   513-move Champions table - 512/513 resolved; the one unresolved id is
   "nomove", @smogon/calc's own placeholder sentinel for an empty move
   slot, not a real move, so Showdown's text tree correctly has nothing
   for it. Note that `desc` for a move with no secondary effect is often
   just "No additional effect." even when its shortDesc separately calls
   out a real mechanical property (e.g. Accelerock's priority) - a genuine
   Showdown convention (desc = additional-effects text, not a full
   Pokedex-style summary), not a data gap here. Move ids already match
   this project's own to_id() convention directly, same as abilities - no
   fuzzy fallback matching needed.

7. Showdown's base data/moves.ts (poke_calc/poke_calc/data/dump/crit_ratios.json -
   each move's own static crit ratio, used by the KO-chance solver to
   blend crit and non-crit damage by the move's real crit probability)
   Source: https://github.com/smogon/pokemon-showdown (unmodified base
   dex's mechanics file, NOT the champions mod and NOT the data/text/
   tree #6 uses - this is moves.ts, a different file with mechanical
   fields, not prose)
   License: MIT (smogon/pokemon-showdown)

   @smogon/calc's own moves.json dump has zero `critRatio` entries -
   confirmed by grep, no crit-chance data exists anywhere else in this
   project. poke_calc/poke_calc/data/generate_crit_ratios.py fetches Showdown's
   data/moves.ts directly and keeps each move's static `critRatio` field
   (defaulting to 1, the base rate, for the 498/513 moves in this dex
   that don't override it) - 15 moves in the Champions table have a
   boosted ratio of 2 (e.g. Night Slash, Stone Edge, Crabhammer, Leaf
   Blade), matching mainline's well-known "high crit ratio" move list.

   Only the STATIC per-move field is captured. Showdown's moves.ts also
   defines DYNAMIC crit-ratio boosts as onModifyCritRatio handlers - most
   importantly Focus Energy's own move effect (+2 stages to the user's
   later attacks) - which are deliberately NOT modeled: they need
   cross-move battle-log state (was Focus Energy used earlier this
   battle?) this stateless, single-hit calculator doesn't track, the same
   category of gap as Aqua Step's dance counter or Alluring Voice's
   "raised this turn" condition (engine/damage.py's module docstring).
   Scope-Lens/Razor-Claw-style items and Super Luck/Sniper-style
   abilities' crit-stage effects are similarly unmodeled for the same
   reason. Move ids already match this project's own to_id() convention
   directly, same as #6 - no fuzzy fallback matching needed.
